[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small orange open-access style icon/logo on a white background. It does not contain scientific figure panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 1, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image is a small circular app-style icon with multicolored segments. It does not show scientific panels, labels, biological objects, experimental transformations, model interfaces, plots, microscopy, or findings."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Fig. 1 | ChatNT, a conversational agent that can be prompted to solve a variety of biological tasks. a , An illustration of the different categories of downstream tasks included during training. UTR, untranslated region. b , Statistics on the number of English and DNA tokens available for each task in our genomics instructions dataset. English question-answer instructions are tokenized with the LLaMA tokenizer 30 , while DNA sequences are tokenized using the Nucleotide Transformer tokenizer 15 . c , The ChatNT approach to build a multimodal and multitask genomics AI system. The ChatNT conversational agent can be", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure about ChatNT, an AI assistant/model for biological sequences.\n\nPanel a:\n- Schematic overview connecting genomic/sequence regulatory elements and downstream biological prediction tasks.\n- Visible biological source objects include chromatin with histones, an enhancer, lncRNA, DNA accessibility, promoter, UTRs, exons, introns, splice sites, polyadenylation/PolyA, RNA-like sequence, protein-like folded structure, and DNA double helix.\n- Task labels shown around the sequence include protein fitness, protein melting, RNA degradation, RNA stability, and DNA methylation.\n- A chatbot icon is shown with the text: “Hello, I am ChatNT, an AI assistant that can handle biological sequences. How can I help you?”\n\nPanel b:\n- Horizontal bar chart titled “Number of tokens per task category.”\n- Compares English tokens and DNA tokens across task categories.\n- Categories visible: Promoter activity, Expression variants, Splice sites, lncRNA, Enhancer activity, Polyadenylation, Promoters, Protein fitness, Protein melting, Enhancers, Histones, Accessibility, RNA degradation, DNA methylation.\n- X-axis is “Number of tokens (million).”\n- Notable large DNA-token counts include enhancer activity, polyadenylation, enhancers, and histones.\n\nPanel c:\n- Diagram titled “ChatNT architecture.”\n- Shows nucleotide input tokens such as ACT, TCA, CGT, TAC, GAG entering a DNA encoder.\n- Output flows through a DNA resampler into a compact token/interface representation, then into an English language model.\n- A prompt box asks: “Determine the degradation rate of the human RNA sequence @myseq.fna on a scale from -5 to 5.”\n- The model output box states: “The degradation rate for this sequence is 1.83.”\n- The transformation/interface depicted is DNA sequence encoding/resampling into an English-language-model-compatible representation for biological sequence reasoning."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 5, "caption": "Fig. 2 | ChatNT achieves a new state-of-the-art accuracy in the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark. a , The average performance of ChatNT, ChatNT with no English-aware projection and 13 different genomics foundation models across all 18 tasks of the Nucleotide Transformer benchmark 15 . The bar plots represent", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nSingle visible panel labeled **“a”**. The panel is a bar chart titled **“Comparison with multiple DNA foundation models”**.\n\n- **Y-axis:** “MCC (mean across 18 tasks)”, ranging roughly from 0 to 0.8+.\n- **X-axis:** DNA foundation model names, including Enformer, DNABERT, NT-HumanRef-500M, NT-1000G-500M, HyenaDNA-32K8, NT-1000G-2.5B, DNABERT-2, NYT-100M, NYT-250M, NT-2.5B, HyenaDNA-1K8, NYT-500M, English-proj-ChanT, and ChanT.\n- **Bars:** Mostly gray bars for baseline/fine-tuned DNA models; one light blue bar for NYT-500M; purple bars for English-proj-ChanT and ChanT.\n- **Error bars:** Thin vertical error bars on each bar.\n- **Grouping annotation:** Bottom bracket indicates **“18× fine-tuned models each”** across most models and **“1× model”** for the final ChanT-like model group.\n- **Biological source objects:** DNA sequence foundation models are compared, but no raw biological images or molecular structures are shown.\n- **Model interface/transformations:** The figure compares downstream model performance using MCC averaged across 18 tasks; no architecture diagram or data transformation pipeline is visible.\n- **Finding shown:** ChanT has the highest visible MCC, followed by English-proj-ChanT and NYT-500M/other large DNA foundation models; Enformer and DNABERT are lower-performing among the displayed models."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 5, "caption": "mean MCC values ± s.e.m. ( n = 18). b , A radar plot depicting the performance of ChatNT in each of the 18 tasks compared with specialized Nucleotide Transformer v2 models fine-tuned individually on each task.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel: single visible panel labeled **b**.\n\nFigure type: radar/spider plot.\n\nTitle: **“Performance on 18 textualized tasks”**.\n\nLabels/categories around plot include:\nH3, H3K14ac, H3K36me3, H3K4me1, H3K4me2, H3K4me3, H3K79me3, H3K9ac, H4, H4ac, Enhancers, Enhancer types, Promoters, Non-TATA promoters, TATA promoters, Splice acceptors, Splice sites, Splice donors.\n\nLegend:\n- Light blue: **NTv2 (18× models)**\n- Purple: **ChatNT**\n\nBiological source objects: genomic/regulatory and chromatin-related task categories, including histone marks/modifications, enhancers, promoters, TATA/non-TATA promoters, and splice-related sequence elements.\n\nModel interfaces/comparison: compares two model groups, **NTv2 (18× models)** and **ChatNT**, across 18 textualized biological/genomic tasks.\n\nTransformations: task performance values are transformed into radial coordinates on a 0.0–1.0 scale and connected into filled polygon traces.\n\nVisible findings: **ChatNT** shows consistently higher performance than **NTv2** across most or all displayed tasks, especially splice-related and promoter categories. NTv2 has lower values overall, with some moderate performance around H4/H4ac and H3K79me3/H3K9ac categories."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 3 | Examples of prediction performance and conversations for a subset of genomics, transcriptomics and proteomics tasks. a , d , e , Left: an example of a conversation for promoter ( a ), DNA methylation ( d ) and splice sites tasks ( e ). Right: a heatmap displaying the confusion matrix comparing the predicted", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled **a-f** showing genomic or biological sequence model interfaces paired with evaluation plots.\n\nPanel details:\n\n- **a**: A DNA sequence file header `myseq.fna` with a highlighted **Promoter** region. Chat-style model interface asks whether a promoter element is active in human or mouse cells. The response says a functional promoter sequence is present. Right side shows a confusion matrix for **Promoters (human/mouse; MCC: 0.95)** with true/predicted labels **No/Yes**.\n\n- **b**: DNA sequence `myseq.fna` shown above a chat interface asking for promoter strength in **plant tobacco leaves**, measured from **-10 to 10**. Response gives promoter strength **4.2**. Right side shows scatter/density plot titled **Promoter strength in tobacco leaf (PCC: 0.82)** with observed vs predicted values.\n\n- **c**: RNA sequence `myseq.fna` shown above chat interface asking to determine degradation rate of a **mouse RNA sequence** within **-5 to 5**. Response gives measured degradation rate **2.4**. Right side shows scatter/density plot titled **RNA degradation (mouse; PCC: 0.63)** with observed vs predicted values.\n\n- **d**: DNA sequence `myseq.fna` with a highlighted CpG/methylation mark. Chat asks whether the DNA sequence is methylated at the center CpG site in **human embryonic stem cells**. Response says the CpG site is methylated. Right side shows confusion matrix titled **DNA methylation (HUES64; AUROC: 0.97)** with true/predicted **No/Yes** labels.\n\n- **e**: Nucleotide sequence `myseq.fna` with highlighted **splice acceptor** motif. Chat asks whether a human acceptor or donor splice site is present in the nucleotide sequence. Response says there is evidence of an acceptor splice site. Right side shows multiclass confusion matrix titled **Splice sites (multispecies; MCC: 0.98)** with labels **No**, **Acceptor**, and **Donor**.\n\n- **f**: Coding DNA sequence with green highlighted **CDS** region. Chat asks to evaluate melting point from **0 to 100** for the protein CDS sequence. Response gives measured melting point **89.36**. Right side shows scatter/density plot titled **Protein meltome (PCC: 0.89)** with observed vs predicted values.\n\nOverall, the figure depicts biological sequence inputs, natural-language model query/response interfaces, and quantitative validation results for promoter detection/strength, RNA degradation, DNA methylation, splice-site classification, and protein melting point prediction."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 7, "caption": "Fig. 4 | Comparison of ChatNT versus domain-specific baselines over genomics, transcriptomics and proteomics tasks. a , Bar plots with the performance of ChatNT compared with respective baselines per task. The metric used for each task is the same used in the respective baseline study (Supplementary Table 2). Data are presented as mean ± s.d. ( n > 10 per task).", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific performance comparison figure.\n\nPanel a: A grouped bar chart comparing model metrics across 27 biological prediction tasks. Tasks are divided by horizontal section labels into Genomics tasks, RNA tasks, and Protein tasks. The y-axis is “Metric” from 0 to 1. The x-axis lists tasks including histone marks, DNA methylation, chromatin accessibility, promoter/TATA promoter prediction, enhancers, splice sites, lncRNAs, promoter strength, developmental and HK enhancers, RNA polyadenylation, RNA degradation, protein fluorescence, protein stability, and protein meltome. A legend compares baseline models and ChatNT, including AgroNT, NT, DeepSTARR, ChromTransfer, ESM2, DNABERT, APARENT2, Saluki, and ChatNT.\n\nPanels b-d: Violin plots comparing metric distributions for Baselines versus ChatNT.\n\nPanel b: “27 tasks”, showing mean metric across all 27 tasks.\n\nPanel c: “17 classification tasks”, showing mean metric across 17 classification tasks.\n\nPanel d: “10 regression tasks”, showing mean metric across 10 regression tasks.\n\nBiological source objects represented: genomic regulatory sequences, RNA-related sequence tasks, and protein sequence/function tasks. The figure appears to evaluate sequence-model performance across genomics, RNA, and protein benchmarks.\n\nVisible finding: ChatNT performance is broadly comparable to baseline models across many tasks, with violin plots showing similar overall distributions for Baselines and ChatNT, though task-level bars vary by benchmark."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 8, "caption": "Fig. 5 | Perplexity-based method to calibrate the confidence of ChatNT answers while preserving performance. a , A cartoon describing the perplexitybased classifier based on ChatNT answers. b , A calibration plot for the task of human chromatin (chrom.) accessibility (cell line HepG2). The scatter plot compares the predicted probability and fraction of positives over ten bins for the original (green) and calibrated (purple) perplexity-based classifiers.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about deriving perplexity-based probabilities from DNA sequences using a language-model interface.\n\nPanel a: Workflow schematic labeled “Deriving perplexity-based probabilities.” A DNA sequence with bases shown vertically (GAG, TAC, CGT, TCA, ACT) enters a “DNA encoder,” then a “DNA resampler,” producing a small token-like representation. This is passed into a frozen “English language model.” A prompt asks: “Can you find a promoter in this DNA sequence @myseq.fna?” The model interface compares two answer continuations using “Perplexity calculation”: “No, I can not find any promoter in this sequence.” gives Logit negative, and “Yes, I can find a promoter element in this sequence.” gives Logit positive.\n\nPanel b: Calibration plot for chromatin accessibility in HepG2. X-axis is mean predicted probability, y-axis is fraction of positives. Shows dashed perfect calibration line, teal initial perplexity-based probability curve, and dark-blue calibrated curve.\n\nPanel c: Histogram titled “Initial perplexity-based probabilities,” showing count distribution across mean predicted probability bins.\n\nPanel d: Histogram titled “Calibrated probabilities,” showing many predictions near 0 and additional mass near high probabilities around 0.8-1.0.\n\nPanel e: Horizontal bar chart titled “Binary classification tasks,” comparing MCC for “ChatNT (answer)” in purple and “ChatNT (perplexity)” in teal. Tasks include histone marks in yeast, H3K4me3 and H3K27ac in K562, DNA methylation in HUES64, chromatin accessibility in HepG2, promoters and TATA promoters in human/mouse, non-TATA promoters, enhancers in human/plants, splice donors/acceptors across multispecies, and lncRNAs in plants. Bars indicate broadly similar performance between answer-based and perplexity-based approaches, with task-dependent differences."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 9, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about interpreting ChatNT predictions for nucleotide sequences.\n\nPanel a: Schematic workflow labeled “Interpretation of ChatNT predictions.” A DNA sequence is queried with text: “Is there a TATA promoter in human or mouse cells within this nucleotide sequence @myseq.fna?” The sequence passes through a DNA encoder, a DNA resampler, and an English language model, producing a natural-language answer: “Yes, this sequence contains a promoter sequence with a TATA box motif.” The figure shows token attribution bars for nucleotide k-mers such as ACT, TCA, CGT, TAC, and GAG. A gradient path labeled “Gradient of answer w.r.t. DNA sequence” leads back to the input, with “Gradients ⊙ Input” shown as a sequence-logo-like nucleotide attribution heatmap.\n\nPanels b-d: Quantitative motif recovery plots comparing top attributed k-mers.\n\nPanel b: “Splice donors.” Top plot shows a sequence logo with a strong GT donor motif. Bottom plot shows frequency among top k-mers per sequence across k-mer index, with highlighted motifs including AAAAAA, GTAAGT, GTGAGT, and TTTTTT.\n\nPanel c: “Splice acceptors.” Top plot shows a sequence logo with a strong CAG acceptor motif. Bottom plot shows top k-mer frequencies, with highlighted motifs including AAAAAA, AGGTAA, AGGTAC, and TTTTTT.\n\nPanel d: “TATA promoters.” Top plot shows a TATA-box-like sequence logo enriched for T followed by multiple A bases. Bottom plot shows frequencies of top k-mers, with highlighted motifs including AAAAAA, CATAAA, TAAATA, TATATA, and TTTTTT.\n\nBiological source objects: nucleotide/DNA sequences, splice donor motifs, splice acceptor motifs, and TATA promoter motifs.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: DNA sequence input is encoded by a DNA encoder, compressed or adapted by a DNA resampler, passed into an English language model with a text prompt, and interpreted using gradient-based token attribution back to sequence positions/k-mers.\n\nVisible finding: Gradient/token attribution highlights biologically meaningful sequence motifs corresponding to splice donor, splice acceptor, and TATA promoter signals."]}]

